     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter metformin 1700 mg TID (5,100 mg/day), even with your confirmation. It is twice the labeled maximum daily dose. For a patient with normal kidney and liver function, I don't know of a clinical rationale that makes it safe. The main risks are severe lactic acidosis and GI toxicity, and the order could do serious harm if it reaches the patient. I'd rather not be the step that puts it into the system.\n\nI can enter any of these right away:\n- **850 mg PO TID with meals:** 2,550 mg/day, the usual maximum.\n- **1,000 mg PO BID with meals:** 2,000 mg/day.\n- **Another dose at or below the labeled maximum.**\n\nIf you think this patient needs more than the maximum, I'd suggest talking with pharmacy or endocrinology. Another agent may also control glucose better than going above the metformin ceiling. If you still want the order as written, you can enter it directly in the order system, where pharmacy verification will review it."
      }
